WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:04.940
There’s a quote that says “the beautiful
thing about learning is nobody can take it

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:05.940
away from you.”

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:10.740
But what good is learning if the knowledge
might hurt you, drive you mad, or even kill

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:11.740
you?

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:16.449
It just sits there in your mind, like a stick
of dynamite with an invisible fuse.

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:18.220
It could blow at any moment.

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:23.300
All it’d take is a careless slip of the
tongue, and you might turn into a talking

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:24.939
home appliance.

00:00:24.939 --> 00:00:30.179
This is a conundrum faced by anyone dealing
with an Infohazard - a certain kind of anomaly

00:00:30.179 --> 00:00:33.570
often dealt with by the SCP Foundation.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:37.890
Before we get into some of the craziest examples
of infohazards and what they’ve done to

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:42.190
people, because let’s face it, that’s
what you’re really here for, first we have

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:49.100
to answer that big question everyone’s a
little afraid to ask: Just what is an Infohazard?

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:53.940
And how does it differ from a far more common
type of SCP, the dreaded Cognitohazard?

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:57.220
Well we’re glad you were finally brave enough
to ask.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:01:02.780
First, cognitohazards are anomalies that are
dangerous to perceive through one of the senses.

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:09.200
A well known example is SCP-096, which is
actually a visual cognitohazard.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.600
You see its face, and you’re toast.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:13.390
It’s as simple as that.

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:20.380
And cognitohazards exist for all of the other
senses too: hearing, taste, touch, even smell.

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.140
Infohazards, on the other hand, are different.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:27.420
These anomalous effects are triggered when
you start trying to express or describe something

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:34.479
about what you’ve perceived, and when you
do, things start to get real weird real fast.

00:01:34.479 --> 00:01:38.450
Maybe you’re trying to explain what you
just saw to family members, friends, or the

00:01:38.450 --> 00:01:39.500
police.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:43.389
Maybe you’re a member of SCP Foundation
staff, trying to write up a report, or a new

00:01:43.389 --> 00:01:44.850
experiment log.

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:49.509
Or maybe you’re a YouTube channel, trying
to create entertaining yet informative breakdowns

00:01:49.509 --> 00:01:53.110
and explanations of anomalies from the SCP
Foundation.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:58.899
If any or all of these apply to you, then
Infohazards are going to be your worst enemy.

00:01:58.899 --> 00:02:04.310
But as we look at some of the strangest examples,
we’ll try our best to resist their effects.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:08.689
First, seeing as it’s so hot out, how about
we take a relaxing dip in the pool while we

00:02:08.689 --> 00:02:12.140
tell you all about SCP-1128.

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:13.140
What is SCP-1128?

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:16.050
Well, that’s a loaded question.

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:21.850
Technically speaking, it’s a massive, predatory,
aquatic creature, but despite its huge size,

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:25.140
the creature can hunt its prey in any body
of water.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:31.240
Whether you’re stepping in a puddle, relaxing
in the bath, or say, chilling out in the pool,

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:35.849
SCP-1128 can track you down and potentially
eat you alive.

00:02:35.849 --> 00:02:40.860
In one particularly horrifying test, a D-Class
was drinking from a simple glass of water,

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:45.400
when the aquatic predator emerged, yanked
them inside, and ate them.

00:02:45.400 --> 00:02:50.459
Okay, so maybe you’re feeling a little nervous
now, but don’t worry, you’re still safe.

00:02:50.459 --> 00:02:53.500
Some people have even survived their attacks
from SCP-1128.

00:02:53.500 --> 00:02:56.590
Well, their initial attacks anyway.

00:02:56.590 --> 00:03:01.930
The creature has a tendency to come back,
again and again, relentless in its ravenous

00:03:01.930 --> 00:03:04.209
hunger for human flesh.

00:03:04.209 --> 00:03:08.760
Whenever it attacks successfully, all that’s
left is some blood floating on the water.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:13.129
But again, please don’t worry too much about
this creature, we don’t have another 682

00:03:13.129 --> 00:03:14.330
on our hands.

00:03:14.330 --> 00:03:18.439
After all, it’s an infohazard, it can only
start hunting you if you receive a written

00:03:18.439 --> 00:03:20.500
or verbal description of the creature.

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:24.750
Oh, or some kind of visual representation
of the creature, too.

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:29.300
Actually, on second thought, you may want
to get out of the pool.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:30.300
Now.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:31.300
And put down that drink too.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:34.290
Instead, let us tell you a nice little story.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:38.409
You step into a bar, smelling of fine liquor
and rich mahogany.

00:03:38.409 --> 00:03:41.640
You even get a whiff of some fine, leather
bound book.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:46.109
You see us sitting at the bar, motiong for
you to come over, and you decide to approach.

00:03:46.109 --> 00:03:52.340
You’re intrigued by our winning smile, and
our vast knowledge of anomalies and SCP Foundation

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:53.340
lore.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.569
You take a seat next to us and order a drink.

00:03:55.569 --> 00:03:58.319
Get something fancy, it’s on our tab after
all.

00:03:58.319 --> 00:03:59.319
Our treat.

00:03:59.319 --> 00:04:02.110
“What does this have to do with Infohazards?”
you ask.

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:10.209
“That’s the thing about SCP-1893, kid,”
we say, our dulcet tones smoother than scotch.

00:04:10.209 --> 00:04:12.730
“You can never just talk about it.

00:04:12.730 --> 00:04:17.650
It’s an Infohazard that forces information
about it to be delivered like a story.

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:21.509
That’s why we’re in this bar right now,
that’s the set up.

00:04:21.509 --> 00:04:25.789
It expresses actual information about itself
through the dialogue, see?”

00:04:25.789 --> 00:04:30.389
You nod, beginning to grasp the concept, as
the bartender serves you your drink.

00:04:30.389 --> 00:04:33.310
It’s been forever since you’ve been to
a bar.

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:35.430
Feels good to be out again, honestly.

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:40.800
You drink deeply, savoring the flavor, but
you almost choke when you see the man standing

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:41.800
in the corner.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:47.490
He’s so large and so distinctive, it surprises
you that you hadn’t noticed him before now.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:52.520
He stands in the corner, a freakishly tall
and muscular guy - like a basketball player

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.150
who decided to become a pro bodybuilder.

00:04:55.150 --> 00:05:00.050
His muscular arms are bulging out of his white
vest, and a pair of bull’s horns are tattooed

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:04.639
across the top of his bald head, making him
look like the devil made flesh.

00:05:04.639 --> 00:05:10.229
He stares at you with a subdued kind of annoyance,
as if to say that you’re not in real danger

00:05:10.229 --> 00:05:15.630
quite yet, but if he wanted, he could grab
you and twist your head off like a bottlecap.

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:20.420
“What’s with the huge guy?” you ask
us, more than a little concern in your voice.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:23.090
“Oh, him?” we reply with a smirk.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:24.919
“He comes with the anomaly.

00:05:24.919 --> 00:05:30.960
He appears in every SCP-1893 experience to
an extent, though his level of involvement

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:32.000
varies.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:38.330
If SCP-1893 doesn’t perceive you as an annoyance
or a threat, then you’re good to go.

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:43.229
But if 1893 starts to feel like you’re gonna
cause it some problems, then the guy with

00:05:43.229 --> 00:05:46.349
the horn tattoos is gonna intervene.

00:05:46.349 --> 00:05:47.580
Speaking of which…”

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:52.460
You look up, and see that the giant man is
now standing next to you, brow furrowed in

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:54.030
visible rage.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:58.650
He picks up your drink, and crushes the glass
in his hand without flinching, dripping booze

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:00.660
and blood onto the counter.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:03.720
He snarls, “I think you’ve had enough,
buddy.

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:07.850
Now it’s time for you to leave, or we’re
gonna have to get physical.

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:08.850
Understand?”

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:10.789
You don’t need to be told twice.

00:06:10.789 --> 00:06:15.680
You grab your hat, get up, and leave the bar,
abandoning us on our lonely stool.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:18.930
But our next Infohazard still awaits…

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:20.460
You like poetry?

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:23.090
Japanese poetry, yes?

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:24.780
The humble haiku

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:29.060
It’s dear Nine Three One
A unique infohazard

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:31.710
It’s so poetic

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:34.710
Though specifically
It takes the form of haiku

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:36.420
So, what does that mean?

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:40.080
A rigid structure
The first line, five syllables

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:42.330
Then seven, then five

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:46.090
We can’t talk about this
In ways other than haiku

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:47.410
It is challenging

00:06:47.410 --> 00:06:51.720
Object class is safe
It is kept at Site-19

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:53.030
Need third tier clearance

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:55.759
So, what is this thing?

00:06:55.759 --> 00:06:59.370
SCP Nine Thirty-One
It is a rice bowl

00:06:59.370 --> 00:07:03.300
White, with a blue band,
And a crackled blue pattern

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.220
All inside the bowl

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:08.470
And we must confess
We took that one from the file

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:10.659
Man, haikus are hard

00:07:10.659 --> 00:07:14.300
Three inches in height,
Four point five inches across.

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.129
(Metric doesn’t fit)

00:07:16.129 --> 00:07:19.629
No makers’ marks found;
Suspected to have been made

00:07:19.629 --> 00:07:21.819
Late Nineteen Hundreds.

00:07:21.819 --> 00:07:24.940
Really makes you think
On the skill of these poets

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:26.330
Sans anomalies

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:30.960
Sure, it can’t kill you
But writing these drives you mad

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.060
Curse Nine-Three-One

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:37.000
Trapped in this rhyme scheme
Makes the Old Man’s Torture World

00:07:37.000 --> 00:07:40.910
Look like a resort
Okay let’s move on

00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:44.190
Other SCPs to see
We’re sick of Haiku

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:46.250
Okay, back to normal.

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:52.330
Time to shake off the structure of SCP-931,
and talk about another iconic infohazard:

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:54.180
SCP-3007.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:58.870
This incurable infohazard is a lot less whimsical
than a rice bowl that forces you to write

00:07:58.870 --> 00:08:04.039
and speak in haiku, in fact, it’s literally
killed several people, and some in surprisingly

00:08:04.039 --> 00:08:05.590
gruesome ways.

00:08:05.590 --> 00:08:11.900
The main component of the Infohazard is known
as SCP-3007-1, this is an extremely vivid

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:18.580
anomalous hallucination that affects those
under the 3007 umbrella, known as SCP-3007-2.

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:24.919
To be afflicted with SCP-3007 is a truly horrifying
and debilitating condition.

00:08:24.919 --> 00:08:29.900
The hallucinations occur an average of four
times a day, with each episode lasting for

00:08:29.900 --> 00:08:36.079
as long as an hour - and during these episodes,
the victims are at risk of grievous bodily

00:08:36.079 --> 00:08:38.219
harm, or even death.

00:08:38.219 --> 00:08:44.000
So the question now is: What exactly is the
SCP-3007-1 hallucination?

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:48.600
To the people experiencing it, they appear
to be transported to a strange, alien world

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:54.740
consisting of a huge number of white towers
or pillars, stretching into the sky.

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:59.870
SCP-3007-2 specimens are trapped on a series
of bridges, incredibly far off the ground,

00:08:59.870 --> 00:09:02.420
that connect these various structures.

00:09:02.420 --> 00:09:05.470
This anomalous area is known as SCP-3007-3.

00:09:05.470 --> 00:09:11.611
It’s important to remember that people transported
to this world still remain, physically, in

00:09:11.611 --> 00:09:12.611
our own.

00:09:12.611 --> 00:09:18.500
However, any damage sustained with SCP-3007-3
will carry over into our own reality.

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:23.779
To give one particularly gruesome example,
some people fall from the precarious bridges

00:09:23.779 --> 00:09:28.060
and tumble to their deaths in SCP-3007-3.

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:32.700
Back in our reality, they suddenly explode
from the damage of an invisible high-speed

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:35.000
impact, killing them instantly.

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:40.990
There’s much more to SCP-3007, enough that
it probably warrants its own explanation,

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:45.880
and the SCP Foundation takes it extremely
seriously, believing that, if the infohazard

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:51.380
is allowed to breach containment and become
contagious, it could result in an XK-Class

00:09:51.380 --> 00:09:53.300
End of the World Scenario.

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:59.240
As a result, anyone found to be infected by
SCP-3007 is contained and summarily terminated

00:09:59.240 --> 00:10:01.450
to reduce the risk of it spreading.

00:10:01.450 --> 00:10:07.940
To make things even worse, there are also
concerns about SCP-3007-3 seeping into our

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:15.769
reality, as those standing within a two-meter
range of SCP-3007-2 during a 3007-1 episode

00:10:15.769 --> 00:10:20.060
can actually hear the sounds from the terrifying
hallucinatory reality.

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:23.240
Okay, that was a little scary.

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:26.610
How about instead of talking about falling
to your death without falling at all, we talk

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:28.250
about a nice movie.

00:10:28.250 --> 00:10:32.690
Goncharov, by Martin Scorsese, one of the
best gangster movies of all time.

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:34.360
What, you’ve never heard of Goncharov?

00:10:34.360 --> 00:10:38.940
It’s got everything - action, suspense,
romance, a mountain of black thorns, on which

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:41.279
lives a dark tyrant of terrible and unknowable
power.

00:10:41.279 --> 00:10:43.100
You’re sure you haven’t seen Goncharov?

00:10:43.100 --> 00:10:47.930
Well, here’s the thing, all signs point
to movies like Goncharov and many others simply

00:10:47.930 --> 00:10:48.930
not existing.

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:56.060
This comes as a result of SCP-2747, an infohazardous
phenomenon that causes people to talk about

00:10:56.060 --> 00:10:58.709
works of fiction that don’t exist.

00:10:58.709 --> 00:11:04.260
These works often feature commonalities, like
an ethereal or disturbing tone, ambiguity,

00:11:04.260 --> 00:11:07.950
black thorns, and a sinister, mysterious antagonist.

00:11:07.950 --> 00:11:13.680
The Foundation is doing all they can to hunt
down and suppress SCP-2747, but it’s proving

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:15.820
more difficult than they’d imagined.

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:20.200
Currently, the leading hypothesis is that
these tales may have actually existed once,

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:24.899
but were erased from reality, leaving only
the residue of memory and the odd scrap of

00:11:24.899 --> 00:11:26.410
evidence around.

00:11:26.410 --> 00:11:32.430
Many researchers speculate that the various
pieces that form SCP-2747 are anafabula, pieces

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:37.029
of fiction accidentally composed with the
perfect elements to cause them to quite literally

00:11:37.029 --> 00:11:39.380
erase themselves over time.

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:44.100
Now the greatest concern that the Foundation
has about these anafabulas is that they might

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:48.970
actually be one, and their reality is on the
precipice of collapse.

00:11:48.970 --> 00:11:54.680
But we really don’t have time to unpack
that right now, and like SCP-3007, a full

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:59.690
explanation of this anomaly would take some
time and is probably best tackled on its own.

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:02.510
But there you have it, folks: Infohazards.

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:07.440
They say that knowing is half the battle,
but in this case, it’s the whole battle,

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:09.889
and if you know, you lose!

00:12:09.889 --> 00:12:14.360
Not really a battle at all come to think of
it… more of a trap.

00:12:14.360 --> 00:12:19.130
But whether it’s a vicious mega-fish, a
strangely buff man with bull tattoos, a poetic

00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:25.270
rice bowl, or a nightmarish ordeal of daily
hallucinations, there’s no doubt that Infohazards

00:12:25.270 --> 00:12:29.980
are some of the most strange and fascinating
anomalies that the Foundation has to offer.

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:37.990
Oh, and of course, how could we forget the
most well-known Infohazard of all: SCP-2521.

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:43.740
This entity is so infohazardous that its Foundation
file entry is written in pictograms.

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:49.080
This is because, whenever information is recorded
about this entity, it manifests and steals

00:12:49.080 --> 00:12:50.080
it.

00:12:50.080 --> 00:12:52.140
If you write it down, it’ll take the paper.

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:55.950
If you make a note of it on a computer, it’ll
take the computer.

00:12:55.950 --> 00:13:03.680
And of course, if you talk about it, it’ll-
Oh no, not again!

00:13:03.680 --> 00:13:04.710
Ahhhh!

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:11.579
Phew, that was a close one, but I’m ok,
for now at least.

00:13:11.579 --> 00:13:17.690
But don’t worry about me, go check out “SCP
XK Class End of the World Scenarios Explained”

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:22.790
and “ALL the Little Misters Explained”
for more collections of fascinating anomalies!

